      <h2>What is new in the fall 2026 lineup</h2>

      <p>Three signals stand out against the spring 2026 reader market. First, the pocketable category — historically dominated by discontinued oddities — now has two mainstream options: the XTEINK X4 Classic (the spiritual successor to the original X4) and the new DuRoBo Krono, which is the first phone-shaped reader in the top ten with a 300 PPI panel and 6 GB of RAM. Second, color is no longer a $400 premium: three of the ten — the BOOX Go Color 7 Gen II, the Bigme B6 Color, and the Kobo Clara Colour — sit at or under $289. Third, “open Android with full Google Play” is now the default for the mid-tier: four of the ten (BOOX Go Color 7 Gen II, Bigme B6 Color, DuRoBo Krono, Musnap Ocean) run stock Android that lets you install Kindle, Kobo, Libby, and Audible side by side.</p>

      <p>For an EPUB-focused reader, that third shift matters most. A reader that runs Kindle, Kobo, and a generic EPUB reader like ReadEra or Moon+ Reader can consume almost any book file you can legally acquire, without the Send-to-Kindle detour. If your library is mostly <strong>DRM-free EPUB</strong>, this also means you do not need to convert to AZW3 or KFX — you just drag the file into the reader app or onto the device over USB.</p>

      <h2>The two genuinely new devices</h2>

      <h3>DuRoBo Krono (new entrant)</h3>

      <p>The Krono is the most interesting device on the list because nothing else combines its four traits: a 6.13-inch E Ink HD panel at 300 PPI, a phone-shaped form factor, 6 GB of RAM with 128 GB of storage, and an Android build branded as a “Focus Hub.” DuRoBo is a Netherlands-based company; the product page describes the device as designed for “eye-friendly reading, audio playback, and AI-powered note-taking.” Headline price on the vendor site is $279.99; Good e-Reader lists $279.99 as well. The “smart dial” referenced in the Amazon listing is the device’s physical scroll wheel — a deliberate callback to earlier iReader and Boox designs, and useful for one-handed PDF scrolling.</p>

      <p>EPUB support is the question that will determine whether this device lives or dies on reader forums, and the vendor page is light on details. The DuRoBo page lists the device as running an unspecified version of Android and as supporting “standard e-book formats” without enumerating them. Given the Android base and the microSD card slot visible in marketing imagery, EPUB, MOBI, and PDF are almost certainly supported through a stock reader app, but until DuRoBo publishes a full format table, treat EPUB compatibility as “expected but unverified.”</p>

      <h3>Musnap Ocean (new entrant)</h3>

      <p>Musnap is the international brand that wraps China’s iReader Ocean line for global distribution — the same relationship that BOOX uses for Onyx’s international SKUs. The Ocean is a 7-inch Android ePaper tablet with an octa-core 2.2 GHz processor, 4 GB of RAM, and 64 GB of storage, and unlike the older iReader devices it ships with full Google Play. Stylus support is via a separately sold Musnap Stylus Pen. Headline price is $259.</p>

      <p>The pitch is a paper-like writing surface for readers who also want to annotate. EPUB compatibility here is straightforward: any Android reader app — ReadEra, Moon+, Lithium — installs from Google Play and opens EPUB, EPUB3, and KEPUB files natively. PDFs reflow on tap. If your library is a mix of EPUB and PDF research papers, the Ocean is one of the better-balanced options in the roundup.</p>

      <h2>What is missing from this roundup</h2>

      <p>Two omissions are worth flagging. First, no Onyx BOOX Tab or Note series device made the list, despite the Go Color 7 Gen II being there. Second, the reMarkable Paper Pro and the Boox Mira-class e-ink monitors are absent — both are aimed at writing and reading workflows that overlap with the Ocean and BOOX Go Color 7, but neither was picked. Good e-Reader’s roundup is a reading-first list; if you need a writing-first device, see our separate coverage of the BOOX lineup. Notably absent on the EPUB side: PocketBook’s Color Note and the new Bigme HiBreak series, both of which we have covered in earlier articles this year.</p>

      <h2>How to use this roundup if your library is DRM-free EPUB</h2>

      <p>If your library is already <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB</a>, almost every device on this list works out of the box. The two exceptions are the Kindle Paperwhite and the Kindle Colorsoft, which both require the <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">Send-to-Kindle</a> workflow plus Amazon’s KFX conversion. For everything else, drag the EPUB file onto the device over USB or sideload it through Dropbox (PocketBook, Kobo) or Google Drive (BOOX, Bigme, Musnap, DuRoBo).</p>

      <p>If your library is mostly Kindle books and you want a non-Amazon reader, the standard recipe is to remove DRM with Calibre’s DeDRM plugin, then <a href="/blog/kindle-to-epub/">convert Kindle to EPUB</a> and sideload. This is a one-time setup per device. For users who want to migrate an old Kindle library permanently, see our <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">migration guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Buying recommendations</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cheapest path to a real e-reader:</strong> XTEINK X4 Classic at $89. The 4.3-inch pocketable form factor is genuinely new, the EPUB support is explicit in the product copy, and the price is the lowest in the roundup.</li>
        <li><strong>Best mid-range color for under $200:</strong> Kobo Clara Colour at $179.98. Native EPUB and KEPUB, no DRM headaches for sideloaded libraries, waterproof.</li>
        <li><strong>Best for Kindle users who want to stay in the Amazon ecosystem:</strong> Kindle Paperwhite 12th Gen at $219.99. Not EPUB-native, but Send-to-Kindle + KFX is now reliable enough that it does not feel like a compromise for personal documents.</li>
        <li><strong>Best Android ePaper tablet:</strong> BOOX Go Color 7 Gen II at $289.99. Full Google Play, color Kaleido 3 panel, stylus support via the BOOX InkSense active pen.</li>
        <li><strong>Best phone-shaped reader:</strong> DuRoBo Krono at $279.99. The only phone-sized reader in the roundup with 6 GB RAM and 300 PPI; the smart dial is a real productivity feature for PDFs.</li>
        <li><strong>Best for writing + reading:</strong> Musnap Ocean at $259. 7-inch E Ink, full Google Play, separately sold stylus; closest competitor to the reMarkable Paper Pro at half the price.</li>
        <li><strong>Best premium color with note-taking:</strong> Kobo Libra Colour at $258.74. Adds page-turn buttons and stylus support to the Clara Colour’s color panel; the standard pick if you want a flagship Kobo.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What to verify before you buy</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Format support.</strong> Every Android device on this list will read EPUB through a third-party reader app, but the bundled reader may differ. Confirm that the app you already use is on Google Play before ordering.</li>
        <li><strong>Warranty and return policy.</strong> DuRoBo and Musnap are newer brands with thinner US service networks than Amazon, Kobo, or PocketBook. If you are outside the EU, check the vendor’s return policy before paying.</li>
        <li><strong>Stylus inclusion.</strong> BOOX, Musnap, Kobo, and Bigme all sell their styluses separately in most configurations. The price you see in a roundup is almost always the reader-only price.</li>
        <li><strong>Storage expansion.</strong> PocketBook, Kobo Clara, BOOX, Bigme, DuRoBo, and Musnap all advertise microSD or USB-C OTG storage. Kindle devices do not.</li>
        <li><strong>Library migration path.</strong> If you have a Kindle library you intend to keep on the new device, run <a href="/blog/kindle-to-epub/">the Kindle-to-EPUB conversion</a> on a desktop before unboxing the new reader.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Fall 2026 is a good time to be shopping for an e-reader. Color is now mid-range, pocketable is back, and the open-Android mid-tier gives you a real choice that did not exist two years ago. If you are coming from an old Kindle or a six-year-old Kobo, the upgrade is worth doing; if you bought anything in 2024 or 2025, the marginal gains are smaller and you may want to wait for the 2027 E Ink Carta 1400 panels.</p>

      <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Which e-reader on this list has the most reliable EPUB support?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>For native EPUB without installing extra apps, the Kobo Clara Colour, Kobo Libra Colour, and PocketBook Era Lite are the strongest picks — all three list EPUB, EPUB3, and KEPUB in their official format tables. The four Android devices on the list (BOOX Go Color 7 Gen II, Bigme B6 Color, DuRoBo Krono, Musnap Ocean) achieve the same result through any installed reader app from Google Play. The two Kindles in the roundup require <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">the Send-to-Kindle workflow</a> plus Amazon’s KFX conversion.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Is the DuRoBo Krono worth buying over the XTEINK X4 Classic?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>They target different use cases. The X4 Classic is a $89 dedicated pocketable reader with physical page-turn buttons and a 4.3-inch panel — strictly for reading, no distractions. The DuRoBo Krono is a $279.99 Android ePaper device with 6 GB of RAM and a smart dial — closer to an e-ink phone than a pure reader. If your goal is “the cheapest reader that does EPUB well,” the X4 Classic wins. If your goal is “an e-ink device that fits in a pocket and can also run reader apps, music, and AI notes,” the Krono wins.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Do I need to convert my EPUB files for any of these readers?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>No, not for any reader on this list other than the two Kindles. EPUB is the standard open e-book format and every non-Amazon reader in the roundup loads EPUB files directly. If you want to convert an EPUB to a Kindle format, see our <a href="/blog/kindle-to-epub/">Kindle-to-EPUB</a> and <a href="/blog/epub-to-kindle-formatting-2026/">EPUB-to-Kindle formatting</a> guides.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">What is the difference between the BOOX Go Color 7 Gen II and the Bigme B6 Color?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Both run open Android with Google Play and both use Kaleido 3 color panels. The BOOX is 7 inches with 4 GB of RAM and 64 GB of storage, runs Android 13, and supports the BOOX InkSense active stylus. The Bigme B6 Color is 6 inches with 2 GB of RAM and 32 GB of storage and runs Android 14. The BOOX is the better reading-and-writing tablet; the Bigme is the cheaper pocketable color option. They are not direct substitutes.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Is Fall 2026 a good time to buy an e-reader, or should I wait?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>It depends on what you already own. If your current reader is three or more years old, the Fall 2026 lineup is a real upgrade — color is now affordable, pocketable is back, and the open-Android mid-tier has matured enough to recommend. If you bought in 2024 or 2025, the marginal gains are smaller; you may want to wait for the E Ink Carta 1400 panels that have been <a href="/blog/e-ink-carta-1400-delayed-2027/">delayed into 2027</a>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
